  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 309 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A stalled boundary resides across the region. Weak low \npressure (barely falling below 1020mb) will try to shift the \nboundary northwards, but with little success due to a trough digging \non the west side of a longwave trough and a strong high pressure \nbuilding into Ontario Province this evening, which will promote \nmore west-northwest flow. So little, if any, latitudinal gain of the \nlow is expected. Good isentropic ascent and a somewhat favorable jet \nconfiguration should at least produce light to moderate rain across \nsouthern Vermont. Otherwise, cool on Friday with highs in the 60s. \nThat'll lead into some frost potential. With the strong high nosing \nin, it looks like Vermont will hold onto some gradient flow, but \nnorthern New York will see it collapse Friday night. There will be \nan upper lobe of vorticity tracking south, and that will likely \nproduce some high clouds. This could put a hold on frost formation, \nbut if it is thin or has breaks, it may still take place. So there \nis some frost noted in our public products, but keeping it fairly \nlimited for now. Saturday will feature similar conditions with a \ncool day in the 60s again. Another cool night is expected, but high \nclouds will again come into play ahead of the next weather system of \nnote and a switch to south winds as high pressure moves offshore.\n\nFor Sunday's system, it will bear a strong resemblance to today. \nAnother weak low pressure will amble east, but struggle to gain any \nlatitude as another upper trough paired with a strong Canadian high \nwill keep moisture shunted south. This one looks a little more \nlikely to get farther north with surface pressures around 1012mb for \nSunday's low pressure. But with high pressure nosing in and stable \nconditions, stratiform rain is expected mainly south, and there are \nno local hydro concerns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An extended period of North Country mid September \nChamber of Commerce type weather is anticipated acrs our fa for days \n4 thru 7. Large scale synoptic pattern shows large 1035mb high pres \nanchored acrs southern Canada with cool-dry north/northeast flow \nexpected. This fast confluent flow aloft wl help suppress any \nheat/humidity, along with best instability well to our south thru \nmost of next week. Given pw values <0.50\" and deep dry layer in \nplace, expect large diurnal range in daily highs/lows, along with \nlarge difference btwn valleys/summits. Daily highs mostly in the \nmid/upper 50s to mid 60s with lows low/mid 30s colder valleys to \nmid/upper 40s near Lake Champlain. As high pres slowly breaks down \ntoward late next week, a return flow of slightly deeper moisture and \nwarmer air develops, along with better potential for rain showers. \nThere still is a large spread in the timing of high pres breaking \ndown and potential return of moisture by late week or next weekend.",
